Roger Watt <rwwatt@watserv1.uwaterloo.ca> writes:
>from aronsson@lysator.liu.se (Lars Aronsson) :
>>I'd like to hear from other Gopher admins, what similar experience
>>they have had, and from all, what you think about games and gophers.
>
>At the institution for which I work, there is a corporate policy
>statement that the computing resources and the internal and external
>mechanisms by which they are interconnected are to be used only in
>support of the research, instructional, and administrative tasks of
>the institution.
>
>Since you asked ... in my opinion, what you are doing is irresponsible
>unless it is confined entirely within your own organization.

I don't buy into this rationale.

If your site doesn't allow game playing, you just make it clear that
"gaming via Gopher" is still game playing.  It's a question of locality;
you can't enforce your local policies on some remote site.  If you don't
want your gopher clients to spawn telnet sessions (such sessions are spawned 
on the *local* host, not the gopher server), you can always modify the 
client code.

If gopher was a commercial product without source availability, I might
agree with you.  Since the source is both free and widely available, there's
no reason not to modify it to fit your local policy.  

