Sued on the grounds of copyright violation.  Playboy magazine is currently
on an active campaign of suing BBSes and shutting them down.  And they
already have a string of court victories and hefty damage awards (see the
recent issue of WIRED magazine for more on this).  There is no free speech
right to violate copyright, and the vast majority of postings on
alt.binaries.pictures.erotica are copyright violations.  Evidently Playboy
and Penthouse want to create their own BBSes, charge big money, and have
people get their GIFs only from them (with suitable are-you-over-18 checks and
VISA accounts billed up front).

If you carry pictures groups, you are in legal danger, not from Moral
Majority types but from the magazines this material has been ripped off
from.  And this danger is no longer theoretical; real BBS operators are
being sued and are losing.

I suppose it's only a matter of time before Sports Illustrated sues the
Washington University at St. Louis (better known as wuarchive.wustl.edu)
for all those scanned-in swimsuit babes.  See, the magazine publishers are
starting to realize that there could be big money in GIFs, and soon, so
will models and their agents.  Why should a BBS be able to sell access to
GIFs of, say, Cindy Crawford without Cindy getting any money for it?

Of course, enforcing strict copyright compliance on GIFs will work about
as well as the music industry's efforts to stop home taping, but it will
become increasingly dangerous to be blatant about passing around pictures
that are not yours.

